<BlogPost {...meta}>
  <p>Every loan officer has a folder of them. The buyer who was pre-approved in the spring and stopped answering in June. The couple who were going to call "after the lease thing gets sorted." The referral who had a great first conversation and then went quiet for two months. Most of us treat those names as lost. After 25 years, I think that is one of the most expensive habits in this business, because figuring out how to re-engage cold mortgage leads is far cheaper than finding new ones, and most of those buyers still need a loan.</p>

  <p>The reason LOs avoid it is not laziness. It is that reaching out to someone who has gone quiet feels awkward. You do not want to be the lender who keeps "just checking in." So you wait for a better moment, and the better moment never comes. This piece is the playbook I use to make that first message back feel useful instead of needy, along with the actual words.</p>

  <h2>Why mortgage leads go cold (it is usually not you)</h2>

  <p>Start with what actually happened, because the reason determines the message. In my experience, a lead goes quiet for one of a handful of reasons:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The timeline slipped.</strong> The lease got renewed, the bonus came late, the job change took longer. They are still buying, just not yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Life got loud.</strong> A new baby, a sick parent, a busy season at work. Buying a home dropped to the bottom of the list.</li>
    <li><strong>They are a little embarrassed.</strong> You told them what to fix on their credit or how much to save, and they have not done it. Replying means admitting that, so they do not reply.</li>
    <li><strong>They got overwhelmed.</strong> Too many numbers, too many options. Silence was easier than a decision.</li>
    <li><strong>They went with someone else.</strong> It happens. Better to find out than to keep guessing.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Notice that only the last one is a real loss. The rest are people who still want a home and simply drifted, and that describes most of any pipeline.</p>

  <div class="stat">
    <div class="num">80%</div>
    <div class="txt">of mortgage leads require nurturing before they're ready to make a decision.</div>
    <div class="src">— Mortgage Bankers Association</div>
  </div>

  <p>If four out of five leads need time before they act, then a lead going quiet is not a signal that the deal is dead. It is the normal shape of the deal. I wrote about how much business leaks out in that quiet stretch in <a href="/blog/the-follow-up-gap">The Follow-Up Gap</a>. Re-engagement is how you get some of it back.</p>

  <h2>Cold is not the same as ghosting</h2>

  <p>One distinction changes everything about what you send. A <strong>ghosting</strong> lead is one you have been actively chasing: three voicemails, two texts, an email, no reply. A <strong>cold</strong> lead is one that was warm and then drifted, usually because nobody reached out for weeks. Most "dead" leads in an LO's pipeline are cold, not ghosting. We stopped, and so did they.</p>

  <p>The playbooks are opposites. With a ghosting lead, another upbeat message makes things worse. You acknowledge the silence, switch channels, and offer a graceful out. With a cold lead, there is no silence to acknowledge, because you were the one who went quiet. What they need is a reason to hear from you that has nothing to do with your pipeline.</p>

  <h2>How to re-engage cold mortgage leads: the playbook</h2>

  <h3>1. Reread the file before you write a word</h3>
  <p>This is the step everyone skips, and it is the whole difference between a message that lands and one that reads like a mass email. Before you reach out, look at your notes. What was the price range? What was the blocker? What date did they give you? Who referred them? A cold lead who gets a message that references their actual situation feels remembered. One who gets "Hi there, still thinking about buying?" feels like a line item.</p>

  <h3>2. Open with something that changed</h3>
  <p>The best reason to reach out is that something is different now. Their lease end date arrived. The rate picture moved in a way that matters for their scenario. A down payment assistance program opened that fits their situation. The car loan they mentioned should be paid off by now. "Something changed" gives you permission to show up without it feeling like you are fishing.</p>

  <h3>3. Ask one question they can answer in a word</h3>
  <p>A cold lead will not write you three paragraphs. Make replying effortless. "Still aiming for spring?" gets answered. "Let me know your thoughts on your timeline and whether you'd like to revisit your pre-approval" does not. One question, low stakes, answerable from a phone in a parking lot.</p>

  <h3>4. Give them permission to say not now</h3>
  <p>People respond to an easy out because it removes the pressure they were avoiding. Telling someone "if the timing isn't right, just say so and I'll check back later" often gets a reply from the person who would have ignored a pitch. And a "not now, maybe March" is valuable. It gives you a date.</p>

  <h3>5. Bring the agent back in</h3>
  <p>If the lead came from a Realtor, the agent has probably gone cold on them too. A quick note to the agent ("I'm reaching back out to the Nakamuras this week, anything you've heard on your end?") does two things. It sometimes surfaces information you did not have, and it shows the agent their referral is still being worked. That second part matters more than most LOs realize. I covered why in <a href="/blog/lo-realtor-relationship-handoff">why the LO–Realtor relationship breaks down</a>.</p>

  <h2>Four re-engagement messages that work</h2>

  <p>Here are the messages I come back to most. Adjust the details, keep the shape.</p>

  <h3>The trigger date arrived</h3>
  <blockquote>"Hi Kai, it's Jay. You mentioned your lease was up at the end of October, so you were on my mind. Are you renewing, or is buying back on the table? Either answer is fine. If it's back on, I can refresh your numbers this week so you know exactly where you stand."</blockquote>

  <h3>The rate picture moved</h3>
  <blockquote>"Hi Kai, quick one. Rates have moved since we last ran your numbers in June, and it changes the picture at the price range you were looking at. Want me to rerun the payment so you can see the difference? Takes me ten minutes."</blockquote>
  <p>Do not quote a specific rate or payment in a cold message. Rates change daily and you have not verified anything about their current situation. Offer the update; give the numbers once they say yes.</p>

  <h3>The "did anything change" message</h3>
  <blockquote>"Hi Kai, it's been a few months, and I know a lot can change in that time. Is a home purchase still something you're thinking about this year, or has the plan shifted? No pressure either way. I just don't want to be checking in about something that's no longer on your radar."</blockquote>

  <h3>The honest exit ramp</h3>
  <blockquote>"Hi Kai, I haven't wanted to crowd you, so this is my last check-in for a while. If buying is still on the horizon, I'm here whenever you're ready, and your file is easy to pick back up. If the plan changed, totally fine. Just let me know and I'll stop reaching out."</blockquote>
  <p>That last message gets more replies than any of the others. When it does not, you have your answer, and you can move the lead to long-term nurture with a clear conscience.</p>

  <h2>What makes re-engagement annoying</h2>

  <p>The buyer's tolerance is lower for a cold re-engagement than for a fresh lead, so the mistakes cost more. The ones I see most:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>"Just checking in" or "bumping this to the top of your inbox."</strong> It asks for their attention and gives nothing back.</li>
    <li><strong>The rate blast.</strong> Sending the same rate alert to your whole database is not re-engagement. It is a newsletter, and cold leads can tell.</li>
    <li><strong>Guilt.</strong> "I haven't heard back from you" puts the buyer on the defensive. Remember the buyer who is embarrassed about not fixing their credit. Guilt confirms they should keep hiding.</li>
    <li><strong>Machine-gun follow-up.</strong> Three messages in three days after two months of silence feels like a collections call.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring a stop.</strong> If someone asks you to stop contacting them, stop, and note it in the file. That is basic respect, and depending on the channel it is also a compliance obligation. Check with your compliance team on texting rules in particular.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>There is a balance here, because the opposite mistake is far more common.</p>

  <div class="stat">
    <div class="num">5+ vs. 1</div>
    <div class="txt">It takes five or more follow-up contacts to close a typical lead — yet 44% of agents give up after a single follow-up.</div>
    <div class="src">— National Sales Executive Association / Inman</div>
  </div>

  <p>Most cold leads did not go cold because someone followed up too much. They went cold because the follow-up stopped. If you want the structure that prevents that in the first place, here is <a href="/blog/5-touch-follow-up-cadence">the 5-touch cadence I run on new leads</a>. Re-engagement is what you do when that cadence slipped.</p>

  <h2>Why this is worth the awkwardness</h2>

  <p>A cold lead you already spoke with is worth more than a new one. You know their situation, they know your name, and there is usually an agent relationship attached. More importantly, the buyer who resurfaces in six months will go with whoever is in front of them when they are ready.</p>

  <div class="stat">
    <div class="num">74%</div>
    <div class="txt">of homebuyers interview only one agent before deciding who to work with.</div>
    <div class="src">— NAR, 2025 Profile of Home Buyers and Sellers</div>
  </div>

  <p>Buyers do not do a lot of comparison shopping once they are ready to move, and the same instinct carries over to the loan. A well-timed, useful message months before they are ready is how you end up being the person they call. Stay silent, and that person is whichever lender happened to email them the week they got serious.</p>

  <h2>The real problem: you can't see which leads went cold</h2>

  <p>The playbook above is not hard. The hard part is knowing <em>who</em> to run it on. A lead does not send you a notification when it goes cold. It just stops appearing in your day, and by the time you notice, it has been quiet for three months instead of three weeks.</p>

  <p>That is the part we built <strong>DealSync</strong> to catch. Every lead lives on a shared deal card, and when a deal has gone ten days with no activity and no follow-up on the calendar, it gets flagged as stalled and surfaces in your Today panel. You do not have to remember who drifted; the list tells you. When you open one, the AI Coach reads your notes and the activity history, tells you whether the lead looks cold or is ghosting you, and drafts the right message for that situation. For a cold lead, that means a value-first re-engagement built around the details in the file (the trigger date, the blocker, the agent's name) instead of a generic check-in. You review it, edit it, and send it.</p>

  <p>And because the deal card is shared, the referring agent can see that their buyer is being worked, even when the buyer is months away. That alone answers the "any update?" text before it is sent.</p>

  <p>A cold lead is not a lost lead. It is a buyer who still needs a mortgage and has not heard a good reason to talk to you lately. Give them one.</p>

  <p>— Jay Miller</p>
